              "content": "# CLAUDE.md\n\nThis file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.\n\n## Project Overview\n\nReth is a high-performance, modular Ethereum execution client written in Rust. It supports both Ethereum mainnet and Optimism (OP-Stack) via a shared, generic architecture. MSRV is Rust 1.88. Edition is 2024.\n\n## Essential Commands\n\n```bash\n# Format (always use nightly)\ncargo +nightly fmt --all\n\n# Lint\nRUSTFLAGS=\"-D warnings\" cargo +nightly clippy --workspace --lib --examples --tests --benches --all-features --locked\n\n# Run all tests (uses nextest, not cargo test)\ncargo nextest run --workspace\n\n# Run tests for a specific crate\ncargo nextest run -p reth-rpc\n\n# Run a single test by name within a crate\ncargo nextest run -p reth-rpc mytest\n\n# Check compilation\ncargo check --workspace --all-features\n\n# Build optimized binary\ncargo build --release --features \"jemalloc asm-keccak\"\n\n# Build Optimism binary\ncargo build --bin op-reth --features \"jemalloc asm-keccak\" --profile release --manifest-path crates/optimism/bin/Cargo.toml\n\n# Ethereum Foundation tests\nmake ef-tests\n\n# Full PR check (lint + docs + tests)\nmake pr\n```\n\n### Dependency / TOML Changes\n\nWhen modifying `Cargo.toml` files, run these before committing:\n```bash\nzepter                # Validate feature propagation across workspace\nmake lint-toml        # Format TOML files with dprint\n```\n\n## Architecture\n\n### NodeBuilder Pattern (Type-State Machine)\n\nThe node is assembled via a type-safe builder in `crates/node/builder/`. It transitions through states:\n`NodeBuilder` -\u003e `NodeBuilderWithTypes` -\u003e `NodeBuilderWithComponents` -\u003e launched node.\n\nKey types:\n- **`NodeTypes`**: Static type config (ChainSpec, Primitives, EngineTypes)\n- **`NodeComponentsBuilder`**: Builds runtime components (Pool, Network, EVM, Consensus, PayloadBuilder)\n- Hooks system: `on_component_initialized`, `on_rpc_started`, `extend_rpc_modules`\n\nChain-specific nodes compose different components:\n- **`EthereumNode`** (`crates/ethereum/node/`): Standard Ethereum pool, executor, consensus, payload builder\n- **`OpNode`** (`crates/optimism/node/`): OP-Stack extensions with deposit receipts, sequencer support, DA config\n\n### Engine Tree (`crates/engine/tree/`)\n\nProcesses Engine API messages (newPayload, forkchoiceUpdated) from the Consensus Layer. Maintains an in-memory chain tree (`TreeState`) of blocks by hash/number and parent-child relationships. Background persistence service handles DB writes asynchronously to minimize latency on the critical path.\n\nSync modes:\n- **Live sync**: Small gaps (\u003c epoch), downloads blocks on-demand\n- **Backfill sync**: Large gaps, uses staged pipeline\n\n### Staged Sync Pipeline (`crates/stages/`)\n\nUsed for initial sync and long-range backfill. Each stage processes a specific aspect of the chain (headers, bodies, execution, etc.) in sequence.\n\n### Storage (`crates/storage/`)\n\nHybrid database: MDBX for mutable state + NippyJar (compressed columnar) for historical/static data. Provider traits (`crates/storage/provider/`) abstract over both backends.\n\n- Use `reth_fs_util` instead of `std::fs` for filesystem operations (better error context).\n- Never modify files in `crates/storage/libmdbx-rs/mdbx-sys/libmdbx/` -- this is vendored third-party code.\n\n### Execution Extensions (ExEx) (`crates/exex/`)\n\nPlugin system for deriving custom state from chain events. ExEx tasks receive `ExExNotification` events (`ChainCommitted`, `ChainReorged`, `ChainReverted`) and must emit `FinishedHeight` events to signal safe pruning.\n\n### Trie (`crates/trie/`)\n\nMerkle Patricia Trie with parallel state root computation. Split into: common types, DB-backed trie, sparse trie, and parallel sparse trie.\n\n### RPC (`crates/rpc/`)\n\nBuilt on `jsonrpsee`. Namespaces: `eth_`, `debug_`, `trace_`, `net_`, `txpool_`, `web3_`, `admin_`. Engine API (`engine_`) lives in `rpc/rpc-engine-api/`.\n\n### Networking (`crates/net/`)\n\nP2P stack with discv4/discv5 discovery, `eth` wire protocol over RLPx, session/peer management, block/header downloaders.\n\n### Key Dependencies\n\n- **revm**: EVM implementation\n- **alloy**: Ethereum types, RPC types, provider abstractions\n- **op-alloy**: Optimism-specific alloy extensions\n- **tokio**: Async runtime\n- **jsonrpsee**: JSON-RPC framework\n- **MDBX**: Primary database (via libmdbx bindings)\n\n## Code Conventions\n\n- **Formatting**: Nightly rustfmt. Config in `rustfmt.toml` (edition 2021, comment width 100, reorder imports).\n- **Clippy**: Strict nursery lints enabled. See `Cargo.toml` `[workspace.lints.clippy]` for the full list.\n- **Logging**: Use `tracing` with targets: `tracing::debug!(target: \"reth::component\", ?value, \"description\");`\n- **Metrics**: Use `metrics` crate: `metrics::counter!(\"reth_component_ops\").increment(1);`\n- **Errors**: Use `?` operator with typed errors. `thiserror` for error definitions.\n- **Async**: tokio for I/O-bound; `spawn_blocking` for CPU-intensive work. Use `rayon` for parallel CPU-bound computation.\n- **Commit messages**: Conventional format (`feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`, `refactor:`, etc.)\n\n## PR Guidelines\n\n- Keep changes focused. Most PRs touch 1-5 files.\n- Label PRs: `A-rpc` for RPC changes, `C-docs` for docs, `A-op-reth` for Optimism changes, etc.\n- CI checks: format, clippy (deny warnings), tests (nextest), docs, cargo deny, zepter, unused deps.\n- Making components generic (replacing concrete types with trait bounds) is a common refactoring pattern.\n- Comments should explain \"why\", not \"what\". Ask: \"Will this make sense in 6 months without PR context?\"\n",
